WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:05.445
Some systems deserve early attention because they are difficult to patch or replace.

00:00:05.445 --> 00:00:09.145
Legacy applications may depend on old libraries, unsupported protocols,

00:00:09.145 --> 00:00:10.789
or hard-coded algorithm assumptions.

00:00:10.789 --> 00:00:15.723
Embedded systems and IoT devices may have limited processing power, small memory,

00:00:15.723 --> 00:00:18.190
fixed firmware, or weak update mechanisms.

00:00:18.190 --> 00:00:23.534
Operational technology may have strict availability requirements and long certification or maintenance cycles.

00:00:23.534 --> 00:00:26.823
Medical devices, vehicles, satellites, industrial controllers, building systems,

00:00:26.823 --> 00:00:30.935
and field equipment can remain in service for many years.

00:00:30.935 --> 00:00:36.279
Their cryptography may be tied to boot validation, remote access, telemetry, updates, identity,

00:00:36.279 --> 00:00:37.924
or vendor maintenance channels.

00:00:37.924 --> 00:00:41.624
Even if a post-quantum replacement is available in software,

00:00:41.624 --> 00:00:45.324
the system may not have the capacity, protocol support,

00:00:45.324 --> 00:00:48.202
or operational window needed for quick migration.

00:00:48.202 --> 00:00:53.136
Cloud services and vendor products also create timelines outside direct organizational control.

00:00:53.136 --> 00:00:58.069
A company may rely on managed identity, SaaS applications, hardware security modules,

00:00:58.069 --> 00:01:01.358
key management services, certificate authorities, mobile device platforms,

00:01:01.358 --> 00:01:02.592
and third-party libraries.

00:01:02.592 --> 00:01:05.059
Quantum readiness therefore includes vendor management.

00:01:05.059 --> 00:01:08.348
Security teams need roadmaps, support statements, implementation timelines,

00:01:08.348 --> 00:01:10.403
validation expectations, and interoperability plans.

00:01:10.403 --> 00:01:14.926
The systems that are hardest to patch should be identified early.

00:01:14.926 --> 00:01:18.626
That does not mean replacing all of them immediately.

00:01:18.626 --> 00:01:22.737
It means documenting dependency, ownership, exposure, expected lifetime, update path,

00:01:22.737 --> 00:01:24.793
vendor status, and business impact.

00:01:24.793 --> 00:01:29.726
Long migration timelines are easier to manage when discovered before a deadline,

00:01:29.726 --> 00:01:34.660
and much harder when discovered after every other system is already waiting.
